*For 2d and 1b, there may be too much quotes. {{User|ChrisTheOstrich}}
:: I note the uncertainty. Firstly, the quotations are present along with direct attribution to the speakers, and full citations. Secondly, in an article which is documenting things that depend on what people think and feel (rather than, say, the date a steelworks was founded), the people involved are critical to the article. That means that the article should correctly give an indication of each authority's specific point of view, using brief samples of their own words. 
:: Nearly all the quotations are in fact very brief, often just 3 or 4 words, to give the smallest possible "flavour" of each person's opinion. The main exception is Andrea Jain's definition of her "religion of consumer culture", where the definition needs to be spelt out in her own words (since paraphrasing a definition inevitably introduces subtle changes to the sense); I've already trimmed the quotation to leave out non-essential verbiage. Another case is Goldberg's quotation of Scaravelli; his use of the quotation demonstrates that a scholar finds the original wording necessary to express Scaravelli's position on asanas as a vehicle for transcendence; I don't see any way of reducing the quotation without damaging the sense. 
