kind.post_text:
: Gourdiehill, the statement you quote begins "For Darwin", i.e. it is Darwin's own view that is being set out here. This is cited both to his ''[[Origin of Species]]'' and to his ''[[The Descent of Man, and Selection in Relation to Sex|Descent of Man]]''. Darwin states directly in chapter 1 of ''Origin of Species'' that 
::{{blockquote|Any variation which is not inherited is unimportant for us".}} 
:Darwin believed that inheritance, variation, and the struggle for existence were all required for natural selection. The statement is correct as it stands. [[User:Chiswick Chap|Chiswick Chap]] ([[User talk:Chiswick Chap|talk]]) 08:51, 26 October 2026 (UTC)

:: ~2026-57264-55, the paper you cite (Lande and Arnold 1983) states that 
::: {{blockquote|Natural selection acts on phenotypes, regardless of their genetic basis, and produces immediate phenotypic effects within a generation that can be measured without recourse to principles of heredity or evolution. In contrast, evolutionary response to selection, the genetic change that occurs from one generation to the next, does depend on genetic variation.}}
:: You are narrowly correct that there can be selection without inheritance, but only "within a generation"; Darwin (as the sentence I've quoted from ''Origin of Species'' demonstrates) was concerned only with what is stated in the second sentence quoted here from Lande and Arnold, that evolutionary response to selection does indeed depend on genetic (i.e. inherited) variation. [[User:Chiswick Chap|Chiswick Chap]] ([[User talk:Chiswick Chap|talk]]) 08:59, 26 October 2026 (UTC)

